			"date": "2023-01-30T19:22:50.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@sil\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>sil</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@jaffathecake\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>jaffathecake</span></a></span></p><p>arguably, a website has actual discrete hypertext pages that are often read only, while a webapp fetches and/or manipulates data while often performing actions</p><p>so really, a webapp that shows content is just a browser-in-a-browser.</p><p>but there are actual webapps that don't show content. stuff like figma maybe</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
